**Parsing HTML using Jsoup**

Here's an example of how to parse an HTML string using Jsoup and extract the outer HTML of a body element:

```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Element;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String htmlString = "<html><body>Hello, world!</body></html>";

        // Parse the HTML string
        Document doc = Jsoup.parse(htmlString);

        // Select the body element and extract its outer HTML
        Element bodyElement = doc.selectFirst("body").first();
        System.out.println(bodyElement.outerHtml());
    }
}
```

In this example, we first create a `Document` object by parsing the provided HTML string. We then use the `selectFirst` method to select the first occurrence of the "body" element in the parsed document. Finally, we call the `outerHtml()` method on the selected body element to get its outer HTML.

Note that this will print the entire outer HTML of the body element, including any HTML tags, attributes, and text content.

If you need more control over the parsing process or want to extract specific elements or attributes from the parsed document, you can use Jsoup's various methods and APIs. For example:

* `select` method: Select multiple elements based on a CSS selector.
* `getElementsByAttribute` method: Get all elements with a specific attribute value.
* `selectFirst` method: Select the first occurrence of an element that matches a CSS selector.

You can also use Jsoup's HTML parsing APIs to extract specific elements or attributes from the parsed document. For example:

```java
// Extract all paragraph elements
ElementList paragraphElements = doc.select("p");

// Get the text content of the first paragraph element
String paragraphText = paragraphElements.first().text();

// Get all links in the document
ElementList links = doc.select("a");
```

These examples demonstrate some of the ways you can use Jsoup to parse and manipulate HTML documents.
